This problem is about permutations.

The number of ways to choose three people from five is the number of permutations of five taken three at a time.

The number of permutations of five taken three at a time is

\begin{align*}
\frac{5!}{3!}
\end{align*}

Therefore, the number of different groups of three that can be made is

\begin{align*}
\frac{5!}{3!}
&= \frac{5 \times 4 \times 3!}{3!} \\
&= 5 \times 4 \\
&= 20
\end{align*}

Therefore, the answer is \boxed{5}.

